if%27%3E3%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%27152%27%20x2%3D%27328%27%20y2%3D%27152%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3C/svg%3E"}, {"id": "mi-1.3-04", "subject": "micro", "unit": 1, "topic": "1.3", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "A student paid a $30 nonrefundable fee to join a club and then finds she dislikes it. When deciding whether to keep attending, a rational decision maker should", "choices": ["keep attending so the $30 is not wasted", "attend only until the $30 has been used up", "count the $30 as a marginal benefit", "count the $30 as a marginal cost of each meeting", "ignore the $30 because it is a sunk cost"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "A sunk cost cannot be recovered whatever she does, so it should not affect the decision. Only future benefits and costs matter.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-1.3-05", "subject": "micro", "unit": 1, "topic": "1.3", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A consumer finds that the marginal utility per dollar from Good X is 5 and from Good Y is 3. To increase total utility without spending more, the consumer should", "choices": ["buy more of X and less of Y", "buy more of Y and less of X", "buy less of both goods", "keep the current bundle", "buy more of both goods"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Each dollar moved from Y to X adds more utility than it removes. The consumer should keep shifting spending toward X until marginal utility per dollar is equal.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-1.3-06", "subject": "micro", "unit": 1, "topic": "1.3", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "The law of diminishing marginal utility states that", "choices": ["each additional unit consumed adds less utility than the one before", "total utility falls as more of a good is consumed", "marginal utility is always negative", "consumers buy less when their income rises", "the price of a good falls as more is produced"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "As consumption rises, the extra satisfaction from each additional unit declines. Total utility can still be rising while marginal utility falls.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-2.1-01", "subject": "micro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.1", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "A binding price ceiling set below the equilibrium price in a competitive market will cause", "choices": ["a surplus", "an increase in quantity supplied", "an increase in the equilibrium price", "a shortage", "no change in the market"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "At a price below equilibrium, buyers want more than sellers offer, so quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-2.1-02", "subject": "micro", "unit": 2, "topic": "2.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "The graph shows the market for a good. What is consumer surplus at the market equilibrium?", "choices": ["$125", "$150", "$250", "$400", "$500"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Consumer surplus is the triangle between demand and the equilibrium price: ½ × 50 × ($20 − $10) = $250.", "image": "data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%27http%3A//www.w3.org/2000/svg%27%20viewBox%3D%270%200%20460%20330%27%20width%3D%27460%27%20height%3D%27330%27%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%27460%27%20height%3D%27330%27%20fill%3D%27%23fff%27/%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2772%27%20y1%3D%27276%27%20x2%3D%2772%27%20y2%3D%2716%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2772%27%20y1%3D%27276%27%20x2%3D%27438%27%20y2%3D%27276%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%276%27%20y%3D%2713%27%20font-size%3D%2713%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EPrice%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27440%27%20y%3D%27316%27%20text-anchor%3D%27end%27%20font-size%3D%2713%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EQuantity%3C/text%3E%3Cpolyline%20points%3D%2772.0%2C171.8%20234.0%2C171.8%27%20fill%3D%27none%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271%27%20stroke-dasharray%3D%275%204%27/%3E%3Cpolyline%20points%3D%27234.0%2C171.8%20234.0%2C276.0%27%20fill%3D%27none%27