rage cost it pulls the average down, and when above it pulls it up, so MC crosses ATC at its lowest point.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-3.2-05", "subject": "micro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.2", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "A bakery earns $200,000 in revenue and pays $120,000 in explicit costs. Its owner gave up a $50,000 salary to run it. What are the bakery's accounting profit and economic profit?", "choices": ["Accounting profit $30,000; economic profit $80,000", "Accounting profit $80,000; economic profit $130,000", "Accounting profit $200,000; economic profit $30,000", "Accounting profit $50,000; economic profit $80,000", "Accounting profit $80,000; economic profit $30,000"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "Accounting profit subtracts only explicit costs: $200,000 − $120,000 = $80,000. Economic profit also subtracts the $50,000 implicit cost, leaving $30,000.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-3.3-01", "subject": "micro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.3", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "The downward-sloping portion of a firm's long-run average total cost curve reflects", "choices": ["diseconomies of scale", "economies of scale", "diminishing marginal returns", "constant returns to scale", "rising fixed costs"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Average cost falls as the firm grows when it enjoys economies of scale, such as specialization and bulk purchasing.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-3.3-02", "subject": "micro", "unit": 3, "topic": "3.3", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "The table shows a firm's output as it changes both labor and capital. When the firm doubles both inputs, it experiences", "choices": ["increasing returns to scale", "decreasing returns to scale", "constant returns to scale", "diminishing marginal returns", "negative marginal product"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Doubling both inputs raises output from 100 to 250, which is more than double. That is increasing returns to scale.", "image": "data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%27http%3A//www.w3.org/2000/svg