s to entry"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Oligopoly has a small number of firms, so each must consider how rivals will react to its choices.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-4.2-05", "subject": "micro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Members of a cartel that agree to restrict output often break the agreement because", "choices": ["cartels always earn negative profit", "demand becomes perfectly inelastic", "each member can raise its own profit by producing more", "the government subsidizes cheating", "marginal cost falls to zero"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "At the high cartel price, each firm can sell extra units profitably, so each has an incentive to cheat.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-4.2-06", "subject": "micro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "In a prisoner's dilemma, the outcome when both players pursue their dominant strategies is one in which", "choices": ["both players are worse off than if they had cooperated", "both players are better off than if they had cooperated", "one player earns all the profit", "the players earn equal and maximum payoffs", "neither player has a dominant strategy"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Each player's individually rational choice leads to an outcome that is worse for both than mutual cooperation.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-4.3-01", "subject": "micro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.3", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "Which of the following is a key characteristic of monopolistic competition?", "choices": ["A single seller", "Identical products", "High barriers to entry", "Perfectly elastic demand for each firm", "Differentiated products"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "Firms in monopolistic competition sell products that are similar but not identical, which gives each a little market power.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-4.3-02", "subject": "micro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.3", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "In long-run equilibrium, a firm in monopolistic competition", "choices": ["earns positive economic profit", "produces at minimum average total cost", "charges a price equal to marginal cost", "faces a perfectly elastic demand curve", "charges a price equal to average total cost but above marginal cost"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "Entry drives economic profit to zero, so P = ATC. Because demand slopes down, that tangency occurs where price is still above marginal cost.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-4.3-03", "subject": "micro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.3", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Firms in a monopolistically competitive industry are earning positive economic profit. In the long run, the demand curve facing each existing firm will", "choices": ["shift to the right", "become perfectly elastic", "become vertical", "shift to the left", "not change"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Profits attract new firms, which take customers from existing firms and shift each firm's demand to the left until profit is zero.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-4.3-04", "subject": "micro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.3", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "A monopolistically competitive firm in long-run equilibrium is not productively efficient because it", "choices": ["earns positive economic profit", "charges a price equal to marginal cost", "produces at an output below the minimum of its average total cost curve", "produces where marginal revenue exceeds marginal cost", "has no fixed costs"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The firm stops where demand is tangent to ATC on its downward-sloping part, short of the minimum point. This unused capacity is called excess capacity.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-4.3-05", "subject": "micro", "unit": 4, "topic": "4.3", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A monopolistically competitive firm advertises to build brand loyalty. If successful, the advertising will make the demand for its product", "choices": ["more elastic", "less elastic", "perfectly elastic", "perfectly inelastic", "upward sloping"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Brand loyalty makes consumers less willing to switch to rivals when price rises, so demand becomes less elastic.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-5.1-01", "subject": "micro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.1", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "The demand for labor is described as a derived demand because it", "choices": ["depends on the demand for the product that labor produces", "is determined by the government", "is always perfectly elastic", "rises when wages rise", "is independent of productivity"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Firms want workers only because of the goods and services those workers produce.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-5.1-02", "subject": "micro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A firm sells its output at $4 in a perfectly competitive market and hires workers at a wage of $30. The table shows marginal product. How many workers should it hire?", "choices": ["1 worker", "2 workers", "3 workers", "4 workers", "5 workers"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Marginal revenue product is $4 × MP: 48, 40, 32, 24, 16. The firm hires while MRP is at least the $30 wage, which is through the third worker.", "image": "data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%27http%3A//www.w3.org/2000/svg%27%20viewBox%3D%270%200%20260%20192%27%20width%3D%27260%27%20height%3D%27192%27%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%27260%27%20height%3D%27192%27%20fill%3D%27%23fff%27/%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2712%27%20x2%3D%27250%27%20y2%3D%2712%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2751.5%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EWorkers%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27171.5%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EMarginal%20product%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2740%27%20x2%3D%27250%27%20y2%3D%2740%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%27.9%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2751.5%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E1%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27171.5%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E12%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2751.5%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E2%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27171.5%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E10%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2751.5%27%20y%3D%27115%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E3%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27171.5%27%20y%3D%27115%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E8%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2751.5%27%20y%3D%27143%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E4%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27171.5%27%20y%3D%27143%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E6%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2751.5%27%20y%3D%27171%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E5%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27171.5%27%20y%3D%27171%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E4%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%27180%27%20x2%3D%27250%27%20y2%3D%27180%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3C/svg%3E"}, {"id": "mi-5.1-03", "subject": "micro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A firm that hires workers in a perfectly competitive labor market faces a labor supply curve that is", "choices": ["upward sloping, so marginal resource cost exceeds the wage", "downward sloping", "vertical", "identical to its marginal revenue product curve", "horizontal at the market wage, so marginal resource cost equals the wage"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "The individual firm is a wage taker and can hire as many workers as it wants at the going wage.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-5.1-04", "subject": "micro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "If the price of the product that workers produce increases, the firm's demand for labor will", "choices": ["decrease", "stay the same", "become perfectly elastic", "increase", "become perfectly inelastic"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "A higher product price raises each worker's marginal revenue product, so labor demand shifts right.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-5.1-05", "subject": "micro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.1", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "A firm's marginal product of labor is 20 and the wage is $10. Its marginal product of capital is 30 and the rental rate of capital is $10. To minimize the cost of producing its current output, the firm should", "choices": ["use more labor and less capital", "keep its current combination", "use more capital and less labor", "use less of both inputs", "use more of both inputs"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Capital yields 30/10 = 3 units per dollar and labor yields 20/10 = 2. Shifting spending toward capital lowers cost until the two ratios are equal.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-5.1-06", "subject": "micro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A large inflow of workers into a competitive labor market will most likely", "choices": ["raise the wage and reduce employment", "lower the wage and increase employment", "raise both the wage and employment", "lower both the wage and employment", "leave the wage unchanged"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "More workers shift labor supply right, lowering the equilibrium wage and increasing the number of workers hired.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-5.2-01", "subject": "micro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Compared with a perfectly competitive labor market, a monopsonist will", "choices": ["hire fewer workers and pay a lower wage", "hire more workers and pay a higher wage", "hire the same number of workers at a lower wage", "hire fewer workers at a higher wage", "pay a wage equal to marginal revenue product"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Hiring one more worker forces the monopsonist to raise the wage for all workers, so it restricts hiring and pays less.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-5.2-02", "subject": "micro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "For a monopsonist, the marginal factor cost of labor curve lies above the labor supply curve because", "choices": ["workers' productivity falls as more are hired", "the firm sells in a perfectly competitive market", "labor is a fixed input", "the product price falls as output rises", "the firm must raise the wage for all workers to attract one more"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "The cost of an extra worker includes both that worker's wage and the raise paid to every existing worker.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-5.2-03", "subject": "micro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.2", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "In a monopsonistic labor market, a minimum wage set between the monopsony wage and the competitive wage will", "choices": ["reduce employment", "have no effect on employment", "create a shortage of workers", "increase employment", "raise the firm's profit"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Up to the competitive level, the minimum wage makes marginal factor cost flat at that wage, removing the incentive to restrict hiring. Employment rises.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-5.2-04", "subject": "micro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A firm hires both labor and capital in competitive markets. It is using the profit-maximizing amount of each when", "choices": ["the marginal product of labor equals the marginal product of capital", "the wage equals the rental rate of capital", "the marginal revenue product of each input equals its price", "total product is maximized", "average product is maximized"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Profit is maximized when each input is hired until its MRP equals its price, so MRP/price = 1 for both.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-5.2-05", "subject": "micro", "unit": 5, "topic": "5.2", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "The table shows the wage a monopsonist must pay to attract each number of workers and each worker's marginal revenue product. How many workers will it hire, and at what wage?", "choices": ["2 workers at $12", "3 workers at $14", "3 workers at $18", "4 workers at $16", "4 workers at $22"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Total labor cost is 10, 24, 42, 64, 90, so marginal factor cost is 10, 14, 18, 22, 26. The third worker's MRP of $22 exceeds its MFC of $18, but the fourth's $18 is below $22. It hires 3 and pays the $14 the supply schedule requires.", "image": "data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%27http%3A//www.w3.org/2000/svg%27%20viewBox%3D%270%200%20277%20192%27%20width%3D%27277%27%20height%3D%27192%27%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%27277%27%20height%3D%27192%27%20fill%3D%27%23fff%27/%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2712%27%20x2%3D%27267%27%20y2%3D%2712%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2751.5%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EWorkers%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27138.5%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EWage%20%28%24%29%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27225.5%27%20y%3D%2731%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EMRP%20%28%24%29%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%2740%27%20x2%3D%27267%27%20y2%3D%2740%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%27.9%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2751.5%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E1%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27138.5%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E10%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27225.5%27%20y%3D%2759%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E30%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2751.5%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E2%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27138.5%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E12%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27225.5%27%20y%3D%2787%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E26%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2751.5%27%20y%3D%27115%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E3%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27138.5%27%20y%3D%27115%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E14%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27225.5%27%20y%3D%27115%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E22%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2751.5%27%20y%3D%27143%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E4%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27138.5%27%20y%3D%27143%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E16%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27225.5%27%20y%3D%27143%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E18%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2751.5%27%20y%3D%27171%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E5%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27138.5%27%20y%3D%27171%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E18%3C/text%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27225.5%27%20y%3D%27171%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2714%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3E14%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%2710%27%20y1%3D%27180%27%20x2%3D%27267%27%20y2%3D%27180%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27%20stroke-width%3D%271.4%27/%3E%3C/svg%3E"}, {"id": "mi-6.1-01", "subject": "micro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.1", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "Which of the following is an example of a negative externality?", "choices": ["A homeowner plants a garden that neighbors enjoy", "A factory's smoke damages the health of nearby residents", "A student receives a scholarship", "A firm raises its price", "A consumer buys a new car"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The smoke imposes a cost on people who are not part of the transaction between the factory and its customers.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-6.1-02", "subject": "micro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "When the production of a good creates a negative externality, the unregulated market will", "choices": ["produce more than the socially optimal quantity because marginal social cost exceeds marginal private cost", "produce less than the socially optimal quantity", "produce the socially optimal quantity", "charge a price that is too high", "create a shortage"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Producers ignore the external cost, so they supply more than is best for society.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-6.1-03", "subject": "micro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A good whose consumption generates a positive externality will be", "choices": ["overproduced, and a per-unit tax will correct it", "produced at the socially optimal level", "overproduced, and a subsidy will correct it", "underproduced, and a price ceiling will correct it", "underproduced, and a per-unit subsidy can correct it"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "Buyers count only their private benefit, so the market produces too little. A subsidy equal to the external benefit moves output up to the optimum.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-6.1-04", "subject": "micro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.1", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "To correct a negative externality in production, the government should impose a per-unit tax equal to", "choices": ["the price of the good", "the firm's average total cost", "the firm's economic profit", "the marginal external cost at the socially optimal quantity", "the marginal private benefit"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "A Pigouvian tax equal to the marginal external cost makes producers face the full social cost, shifting supply to marginal social cost.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-6.1-05", "subject": "micro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "The graph shows a market with an externality. Which of the following is true?", "choices": ["There is a positive externality, and the socially optimal quantity is Q2.", "There is a negative externality, and the market overproduces at Q2.", "There is a positive externality, and the socially optimal quantity is Q1.", "There is a negative externality, and the socially optimal quantity is Q1.", "There is no externality."], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Marginal social benefit lies above marginal private benefit, which is a positive externality. The optimum is where MSB meets MSC, at Q2, beyond the market quantity Q1.", "image": "data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%27http%3A//www.w3.org/2000/svg%27%20viewBox%3D%270%200%20460%20330%27%20width%3D%27460%27%20height%3D%27330%27%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%27460%27%20height%3D%27330%