27281%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27218.6%27%20y%3D%27296%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2712.5%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EQ1%3C/text%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%27256.4%27%20y1%3D%27276%27%20x2%3D%27256.4%27%20y2%3D%27281%27%20stroke%3D%27%23000%27/%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%27256.4%27%20y%3D%27296%27%20text-anchor%3D%27middle%27%20font-size%3D%2712.5%27%20font-family%3D%27Georgia%2CTimes%20New%20Roman%2Cserif%27%3EQ2%3C/text%3E%3C/svg%3E"}, {"id": "mi-6.1-06", "subject": "micro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.1", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A market is allocatively efficient when", "choices": ["producer surplus is maximized", "marginal social benefit equals marginal social cost", "price equals average total cost", "consumer surplus is zero", "marginal private cost exceeds marginal social cost"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "At MSB = MSC, every unit whose benefit to society exceeds its cost is produced, and no others.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-6.2-01", "subject": "micro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.2", "difficulty": "easy", "stem": "A public good is one that is", "choices": ["rival and excludable", "rival and nonexcludable", "nonrival and nonexcludable", "nonrival and excludable", "provided only by the government"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Public goods are nonrival, so one person's use does not reduce another's, and nonexcludable, so non-payers cannot be kept out.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-6.2-02", "subject": "micro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "The free-rider problem explains why", "choices": ["monopolies charge high prices", "price ceilings cause shortages", "taxes create deadweight loss", "firms advertise", "private markets tend to underprovide public goods"], "answer": 4, "explanation": "People can enjoy a public good without paying for it, so private firms cannot collect enough revenue to provide it.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-6.2-03", "subject": "micro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "Fish in the open ocean are an example of which type of good?", "choices": ["Public good", "Private good", "Club good", "Common resource", "Inferior good"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Fish are rival, since one person's catch leaves fewer for others, but nonexcludable, since no one can easily be kept from fishing.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-6.2-04", "subject": "micro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A Lorenz curve that moves farther away from the line of perfect equality indicates that", "choices": ["income inequality has decreased", "the Gini coefficient has fallen", "income inequality has increased", "average income has risen", "the tax system has become more progressive"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The line of equality represents a perfectly equal distribution, so a curve bowing farther from it shows greater inequality.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-6.2-05", "subject": "micro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.2", "difficulty": "medium", "stem": "A Gini coefficient of zero means that", "choices": ["one person receives all income", "income is distributed perfectly equally", "the tax system is regressive", "the Lorenz curve is vertical", "there is no income in the economy"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The Gini coefficient ranges from 0, perfect equality, to 1, where one person receives all income.", "image": ""}, {"id": "mi-6.2-06", "subject": "micro", "unit": 6, "topic": "6.2", "difficulty": "hard", "stem": "Under a progressive income tax,", "choices": ["higher-income earners pay a larger percentage of their income", "everyone pays the same amount of tax", "everyone pays the same percentage of income", "lower-income earners pay a larger percentage of their income", "tax revenue does not depend on income"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A progressive tax takes a rising share of income as income increases, which tends to reduce after-tax inequality.", "image": ""}]}
